-- and so do the two media, on one molecule with one configuration. This is the result the other three
-- cases could not give. Valine, leucine and isoleucine each have one true count, and the bond graph gets
-- it wrong; glycerol has no single true count. Four, five and eight are all correct answers to "how many
-- proton signals does glycerol have", under the bond graph, in an achiral solvent and in a chiral
-- solvent, and the three are forced by the same two definitions with three groups of operations. So the
-- signal count is not a property of the molecule. It is a property of the molecule together with what is
-- around it, and a record that stores a spectrum against a structure, with no field for the medium's
-- handedness, has stored something that does not determine what it claims to determine. The field's own
-- practice already knows this: it adds a chiral solvating agent or a chiral derivatizing agent precisely
-- to make enantiotopic groups separate, and glycerol kinase makes the same separation without any
-- spectrometer, by being a chiral environment itself.
gl-achiral-not-chiral : class-count Glyc gl-eq gl-rank gl-achiral-gens 14 gl-h ≡ class-count Glyc gl-eq gl-rank gl-chiral-gens 14 gl-h → Empty
gl-achiral-not-chiral q = five-not-eight (concat (sym gl-signals-achiral) (concat q gl-signals-chiral))
